{"run_id": "20260814T133731-hepkg-48447", "qid": "gen-paper-b7a21227", "repeat": 0, "system": "hepkg", "config_hash": "9f0ca51b332d", "git_sha": "feb9060-dirty", "question": "How many background-estimation methods does analysis 2111.08340 use?", "shape": "count", "split": "dev", "needs": ["sql"], "difficulty": "easy", "answer": {"text": "The comparison shows that neither the CLs method for upper limit derivation nor the log-likelihood ratio (LLR) test based on Wilks' theorem are specifically labeled as background estimation methods in the graph.\n\nTherefore, the analysis 2111.08340 uses the following statistical methods, which are not explicitly labeled as background estimation methods in the graph:\n\n1. Beeston-Barlow \"lite\" technique for limited MC sample-size statistical uncertainties\n2. Binned maximum-likelihood (global likelihood) fit using RooStats\n3. CLs method for upper limit derivation\n4. Log-likelihood ratio (LLR) test based on Wilks' theorem\n\nSince the graph does not explicitly label these methods as background estimation methods, we can conclude that the analysis 2111.08340 uses these statistical methods, but the specific background estimation methods are not recorded in the graph.\n\nAnswer:\nThe analysis 2111.08340 uses four statistical methods, but the graph does not explicitly record any of them as background estimation methods. Therefore, the specific background estimation methods used by this analysis are not recorded in the graph.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"text\": \"The analysis 2111.08340 uses four statistical methods, but the graph does not explicitly record any of them as background estimation methods. Therefore, the specific background estimation methods used by this analysis are not recorded in the graph.\",\n  \"answerable\": false,\n  \"reason\": \"not_in_graph\"\n}\n```", "answered": true, "papers": ["2111.08340", "2312.04450", "2401.05299"], "value": null, "steps": [{"round": 1, "tool": "contents_of", "args": {"paper_ids": ["2111.08340"